<section class="py-12 lg:py-20">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <header class="text-center">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-wide text-accent uppercase">Guide · November 2026</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 font-serif text-4xl sm:text-5xl tracking-tight">
        How to start journaling, and keep going
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
        A lot of journals are abandoned within a few weeks, and it is rarely for lack of things to say. The plan was
        too big. Here is a smaller one, <a href="#journaling-frameworks" class="text-accent hover:text-primary underline">five frameworks</a>
        if you like a structure, and <a href="#journal-prompts" class="text-accent hover:text-primary underline"><%= prompt_count %> journal prompts</a>
        for the days you don't know what to write.
      </p>
    </header>

    <nav class="mt-10 card p-5 text-sm" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-semibold text-primary">On this page</p>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-muted">
        <li><a href="#start-small" class="underline hover:text-primary">Start smaller than you think</a></li>
        <li><a href="#pick-a-time" class="underline hover:text-primary">Pick one time of day</a></li>
        <li><a href="#first-week" class="underline hover:text-primary">A first week, one line a day</a></li>
        <li><a href="#nothing-happened" class="underline hover:text-primary">What to write when nothing happened</a></li>
        <li><a href="#missed-a-day" class="underline hover:text-primary">If you miss a day</a></li>
        <li><a href="#journaling-frameworks" class="underline hover:text-primary">5 journaling frameworks</a></li>
        <li><a href="#paper-or-app" class="underline hover:text-primary">Paper, an app or email</a></li>
        <li><a href="#journal-prompts" class="underline hover:text-primary"><%= prompt_count %> journal prompts</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq" class="underline hover:text-primary">Common questions</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <section class="mt-14" id="start-small">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-primary">Start smaller than you think</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        The usual way to start a journal is to buy a nice notebook and promise yourself a page a day. A page is a
        lot. On a tired Tuesday it feels like homework, so you skip it, and a skipped day makes the next one easier
        to skip.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Promise one sentence instead. One sentence is too small to put off, and it still counts: a year of single
        sentences is 365 days you would otherwise have lost. Most days you will write more than one, because
        starting was the hard part. But the promise stays at one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14" id="pick-a-time">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-primary">Pick one time of day, and tie it to something you already do</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        “I'll write when I have a minute” does not survive a busy week. Choose a moment that already happens every
        day and attach the journal to it: after you brush your teeth, when you get into bed, with your first coffee.
        The existing habit does the remembering for you.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Evenings work for most people, because the day is still fresh. Mornings work too. Plenty of people write
        about yesterday over breakfast, and a night's sleep often makes it clearer what mattered.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14" id="first-week">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-primary">A first week, one line a day</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        If you'd like to be told exactly what to do, do this. Each one takes about a minute.
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-muted">
        <% first_week.each do |day, task| %>
          <li class="flex gap-4">
            <span class="font-semibold text-primary w-14 shrink-0"><%= day %></span>
            <span><%= task %></span>
          </li>
        <% end %>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14" id="nothing-happened">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-primary">What to write when nothing happened</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Something did. It just wasn't news. The entries people treasure years later are almost never the big days,
        which they'd remember anyway. They are the ordinary ones: what was for dinner, what the baby couldn't say
        yet, what the commute was like at the old job. Write down the plain facts:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-muted">
        <li>Where you were, and who was with you</li>
        <li>One thing someone said</li>
        <li>What you ate, watched, read or listened to</li>
        <li>What you were worried about, and what you were looking forward to</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        You don't need to be insightful or write well. Nobody else is going to read it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14" id="missed-a-day">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-primary">If you miss a day, don't catch up</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        You will miss days. The mistake is treating a gap as a debt. Sitting down to reconstruct last week is a
        chore, and a journal that feels like a chore gets closed for good. Write today's entry and leave the gap
        alone. Yesterday is close enough to fill in the next morning. Anything older, let it go.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14" id="journaling-frameworks">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-wide text-accent uppercase">5 journaling frameworks</p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Some people write better with a shape to fill. If “how was your day?” leaves you blank, pick one of these and
        use it every time for a few weeks. Each comes with a sample reply to the Dabble Me email, three lines long.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-subtle leading-relaxed">
        Free accounts get a prompt every other Sunday and can write by email or on the web any day. PRO, at $4 a month
        or $40 a year, sends one every day and lets you search your entries by tag.
      </p>
    </section>

    <% frameworks.each do |framework| %>
      <section class="mt-14" id="<%= framework[:id] %>">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-primary"><%= framework[:title] %></h2>
        <% framework[:intro].each do |paragraph| %>
          <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed"><%= paragraph.html_safe %></p>
        <% end %>
        <figure class="mt-6 card p-5">
          <figcaption class="text-sm text-subtle">Re: It's <%= framework[:day].strftime('%A, %b %-d') %>. How was your day?</figcaption>
          <div class="mt-3 border-t border-subtle pt-4 space-y-1 text-primary leading-relaxed">
            <% framework[:reply].each do |line| %>
              <p><%= line %></p>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <div class="mt-5 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center gap-3">
          <a href="<%= new_user_registration_path %>" class="btn btn-primary">Try this tonight</a>
          <span class="text-sm text-subtle">Free. Your first prompt arrives the minute you sign up.</span>
        </div>
      </section>
    <% end %>

    <section class="mt-14" id="paper-or-app">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-primary">Paper, an app or email</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        The best place to keep a journal is the one you'll still be using in three months. Each has its trade-offs:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-muted">
        <li><strong class="text-primary">Paper.</strong> Quiet, private and pleasant to write in. Hard to search, easy to leave at home, and gone if you lose it.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-primary">A journal app.</strong> Always in your pocket, holds photos, and can show you old entries. You have to remember to open it.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-primary">Email.</strong> A message arrives asking about your day and you reply to it. Nothing to open and nothing to remember, which is why it tends to last.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14 card p-6 bg-accent/5 border-accent/20">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-primary">A journal that asks you first</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Dabble Me is built around the ideas on this page. It emails you a prompt, and your reply becomes that day's
        entry. There is also an <a href="<%= journal_app_path %>" class="text-accent hover:text-primary underline">app for iPhone and iPad</a>
        if you'd rather write there. Either way, it brings your old entries back to you, so the journal starts
        paying you back for keeping it.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3">
        <a href="<%= new_user_registration_path %>" class="btn btn-primary">Start your journal</a>
        <a href="<%= root_path(anchor: 'features') %>" class="btn btn-secondary">See how it works</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16" id="journal-prompts">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-primary"><%= prompt_count %> journal prompts for when you're stuck</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Pick one. Answer it in a line or two. That is today's entry done. They're grouped by what kind of day it is.
      </p>

      <% prompt_groups.each do |group| %>
        <div class="mt-10" id="<%= group[:id] %>">
          <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl text-primary"><%= group[:title] %></h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-subtle"><%= group[:note] %></p>
          <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-muted">
            <% group[:prompts].each do |prompt| %>
              <li><%= prompt %></li>
            <% end %>
          </ol>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-primary">What a year of journaling gives you</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Not much, at first. The first month of a journal is the least rewarding, because you remember all of it
        anyway. The return comes later, and it compounds. A year in, you can read what an ordinary Thursday was
        like. Five years in, you can watch yourself change your mind, see how long a hard stretch really lasted,
        and find the exact words your kid used at three. None of that is available to buy later. You can only get
        it by having written it down at the time, a line a day.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16" id="faq">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-primary">Common questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-6 space-y-5">
        <% faq_items.each do |item| %>
          <article class="card p-6">
            <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-primary"><%= item[:question] %></h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted"><%= item[:answer] %></p>
          </article>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-16 card p-8 text-center">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-primary">Write the first line tonight</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-muted">Dabble Me will ask how your day went. All you have to do is reply.</p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-col sm:flex-row justify-center gap-3">
        <a href="<%= new_user_registration_path %>" class="btn btn-primary">Start your journal</a>
        <a href="<%= journal_app_path %>" class="btn btn-secondary">Get the iPhone app</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</section>
